WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.310
Disney's latest animated movie takes us to
the mammal-centric city of Zootopia, where

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:11.480
a con-artist fox and a rabbit rookie cop team
up to crack a mysterious case of missing mammals.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:16.230
I'm Jan and this is 25 fun facts you probably
didn't know about Zootopia.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:21.410
To get inspiration for the character of rookie
rabbit police officer Judy Hopps, the filmmakers

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:26.599
not only visited a wildlife rescue centre,
they even had live rabbits in the studio!

00:00:26.599 --> 00:00:30.920
And observing those real-life rabbits really
did help the animators, for example, they

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:36.300
noticed how rabbits' ears often turn towards
a sound before they do and how and when their

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:37.420
noses twitch.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:42.219
For the outfits Judy wears, the animators
took cues from survivalist gear, the kind

00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:46.609
of clothes worn by Navy SEALs, as well as
leg protection worn by racehorses.

00:00:46.609 --> 00:00:52.449
As for the violet colour of Judy's eyes, that
was actually chosen after the animators decided

00:00:52.449 --> 00:00:57.079
Judy's grey fur was too dull and needed a
little purple adding to make it pop so it

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:00.699
reflected Judy's sparky personality.

00:01:00.699 --> 00:01:05.390
When they were creating the character of scam-artist
fox Nick Wilde, the filmmakers were inspired

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:09.890
by actor Cary Grant, and for Judy Hopps they
channelled characters such as Superman and

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:14.060
Leslie Knope from TV comedy Parks and Recreation.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:18.230
Although voice actors for animated movies
typically record their parts separately, for

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:22.759
their lead roles as Judy Hopps and Nick Wilde,
Once Upon A Time star Ginnifer Goodwin and

00:01:22.759 --> 00:01:27.420
Horrible Bosses star Jason Bateman recorded
as much of their dialogue as possible together

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.759
which helped them get great chemistry between
their characters.

00:01:30.759 --> 00:01:36.220
As a reference to help them animate the scene
where Nick Wilde tries to eat a teeny tiny

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:41.060
piece of cake, the artists filmed Disney and
Pixar Chief Creative Officer John Lasseter

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:46.180
using a tiny fork to eat a very small slice
of cake off a little coin!

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:52.520
After meeting pop star Shakira who plays Zootopia's
own pop star Gazelle, the animators made revisions

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:53.920
to the design of her character.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.229
For example, Shakira suggested that Gazelle
should be curvier, so the artists made her

00:01:58.229 --> 00:01:59.229
hips bigger.

00:01:59.229 --> 00:02:03.939
They also made Gazelle's eyes darker, her
lashes longer, her hair wavier, and gave her

00:02:03.939 --> 00:02:09.180
an outfit similar to one Shakira wore for
one of her performances.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.170
Officer Benjamin Clawhauser was initially
going to be called Hugo and some original

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.770
concept art shows him wearing an Hawaiian
shirt very similar to the one Nick Wilde wears

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.100
in the final movie.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.640
The filmmakers wanted to make sure that each
of the movie's animals was based on their

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:24.820
real-world behaviour.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:30.000
So, they spent 18 months researching a variety
of animals, finding out how they interact,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.320
socialise, and build communities in the wild.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:37.070
They spoke to experts from all over the world
and visited Disney's Animal Kingdom, San Diego

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:40.550
Zoo Safari Park, and the Natural History Museum
of Los Angeles County.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:46.910
And they even spent two weeks in Kenya learning
all about animal personality and behaviour.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:50.900
The filmmakers' research also helped them
decide which roles to give to each animal

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.270
in the world of Zootopia.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:56.380
For example, they found out that cape buffaloes
are tough and relentless, so they opted to

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:59.620
make Zootopia's chief of police a buffalo.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:04.370
During their trip to Africa, the filmmakers
noticed that whenever they drove by a herd

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:08.580
of buffaloes, the buffaloes would stop what
they were doing and fix them with their gaze.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:13.220
That gave the animators the idea to give Chief
Bogo that same stare when he looks at Nick

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.010
Wilde.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:19.900
The filmmakers named the head of the Zootopia
Police Department Chief Bogo after the Swahili

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:23.560
word m'bogo which means cape buffalo.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:28.150
According to Art director Cory Loftis, when
they were designing Bogo, the filmmakers researched

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:31.980
police officers who are also bodybuilders
and discovered that they could not fasten

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:37.010
the top button on their shirts, so they took
that idea for Chief Bogo.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:41.180
Because Assistant Mayor Bellwether is a sheep,
the animators decided that all of her six

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.480
outfits should be made of wool!

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.940
And on her orange skirt, you'll even spot
a scissor pattern, which represents shears!

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:52.650
For the scene where Nick Wilde touches the
big tuft of wool on Bellwether's head to see

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:57.380
how soft it is, the artists had to create
new technology as touching fur is normally

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:03.400
something they tend to avoid as it's especially
complex in computer-generated animation.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:09.010
Speaking of fur, each mouse you see in Zootopia
has around 400,000 strands of hair, which

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:12.160
is the same number of strands that made up
Elsa's hair in Frozen!

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.739
And, in case you're wondering what that means
for the larger animals, well, a giraffe has

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:18.430
9.2 million hairs!

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:23.980
For the design of Mayor Leodore Lionheart,
the animators took inspiration from Mufasa

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:25.520
in Disney's The Lion King.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:29.330
And they used technology they'd developed
when making Frozen to animate his huge mane

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.090
of hair.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:35.350
The clothes worn by the Ottertons are another
example of the level of detail the animators

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:38.340
went to when they were designing the animals'
outfits in the movie.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:42.540
The Ottertons wear thick fisherman-knit sweaters
with little fish on them.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:48.280
Early on in the animation process, Disney
story artist Raymond Persi recorded a temporary

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:53.280
voice track for the character of Flash, who
works at the DMV, aka the Department of Mammal

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:54.280
Vehicles.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.120
But the filmmakers and Chief Creative Officer
John Lasseter loved Persi's performance so

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:02.040
much that they decided to keep it for the
final film.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:06.440
According to the filmmakers, to design Zootopia,
they had to 'think like animals'!

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:10.590
In other words, because the city of Zootopia
was built by the animals who live there, the

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:14.490
animators began by asking themselves, given
the same technology and knowledge as humans,

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:19.060
what would a mammal metropolis look like if
it were made by animals?!

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.620
The design of cities from around the world
also inspired the animators.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:27.720
As well as looking to New York and London,
artists were equally inspired by Russian architecture,

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:31.970
which you can see in the onion-shaped domes
of Tundratown.

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:36.410
And Madrid's Atocha railway station was the
inspiration behind the interior tropical garden

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:39.180
at Zootopia's train station.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:43.340
The idea for Sahara Square, which contains
sand dunes as well as buildings shaped like

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:46.920
sand dunes, was influenced by Monte Carlo
and Dubai.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:50.830
Because many desert animals are nocturnal,
the animators designed lots of night-time

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:57.080
activities such as casinos as well as a huge
palm-tree hotel with an oasis around it.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:01.830
Savanna Central, which is Zootopia's central
hub and home to the city's police department,

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:07.400
train station and city hall, was partly based
on Disneyland's hub-and-spoke design.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:12.620
Advances in technology allowed the filmmakers
to create incredibly detailed environments

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:17.780
including over 500,000 trees in the city's
Rain Forest District, each of which has around

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.750
30,000 leaves on it!

